.\" Man page for lookit, a modern TUI browser for the finger protocol.
.\" Preview it without installing:  man ./man/lookit.1
.\"
.\" Keep this readable as prose. It is the reference for people on a tilde or
.\" pubnix box who have no browser to hand, so it explains rather than lists.
.\" Deliberately absent: the keybindings. "?" inside lookit is context-aware and
.\" derived from the live keymap, so any list printed here would start rotting
.\" the day a binding moves.
.TH LOOKIT 1 2026-08-21 lookit "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lookit \- modern TUI browser for the finger protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lookit
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B lookit
.BR \-v | \-\-version
.br
.B lookit
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
lookit is a modern TUI browser for the finger protocol. It opens full-screen:
the list of users on a host is selectable, links inside a response can be
followed, and you can walk back through everywhere you have been without
fetching any of it again.
.PP
Finger (RFC 1288, TCP port 79) is one of the oldest things on the internet: ask
a server about a person and it tells you who they are, whether they are logged
in, and whatever they have left in their \&.plan file. It faded when the web
arrived but never died. Tilde communities and public access unix systems still
run finger servers, and people still keep a \&.plan as a slow, personal sort of
microblog.
.BR finger (1)
is built around asking about one address; lookit is for looking around when you
do not know where you are going.
.PP
Run it with no arguments and it opens on the startpage \(em a built-in catalog
of finger communities and services, with your own bookmarks above it. Give it a
target and it opens there instead, with the startpage one keypress away.
.PP
lookit only reads. It does not post, does not edit, does not poll, and does not
answer anybody's queries; serving a \&.plan of your own is
.BR fingerd (8)'s
job.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and build date and exit.
.PP
That is the entire command line. Everything else happens inside the browser,
where pressing \fB?\fR shows the keys that work on the screen you are currently
looking at.
.SH TARGETS
At most one target may be given.
.TP
.I user@host
Look up one person.
.TP
.I @host
Ask a host who is around. When it answers with something lookit recognises as a
list of users, that list opens for browsing; when it does not, the response is
shown as it came.
.TP
.I finger://host/user
A finger URL, of the sort you find inside a response.
.PP
Any of these may carry an explicit port, as in
.IR @tilde.team:79 ;
79 is the default and rarely needs saying. One-relay forwarding
.RI ( user@host@relay )
works. Longer relay chains do not, and lookit will tell you so rather than
guess.
.PP
A blank or malformed target is not a usage error: lookit still opens, and shows
you what was wrong with it where you can edit it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lookit/bookmarks
Your bookmarks, and the only configuration lookit has. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file lives at
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lookit/bookmarks
instead.
.PP
This file is meant to be edited by hand, and lookit treats it that way: your
comments, blank lines, indentation and ordering all survive anything it writes.
One target per line, optionally followed by the date you were last there.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
jonathan@tilde.team 2026\-08\-14
@plan.cat
weather@bbs.airandwave.net   # a note to yourself
.EE
.RE
.PP
Anything after a
.B #
is a comment. lookit keeps it exactly as you wrote it, and never displays it.
.PP
lookit fills in those dates itself, whenever you visit somewhere you have
already bookmarked. Visiting the same place twice in one day changes nothing,
so the file is left alone.
.SS Settings
These settings can appear on a line of their own, anywhere in the file.
.TP
.B catalog off
Hide the built-in catalog, leaving the startpage as your own bookmarks and
nothing else.
.B catalog on
brings it back, and is the default.
.TP
.B sort manual
Show your bookmarks in the order the file lists them. By default lookit shows
them longest ago first, so whatever you have been neglecting is at the top, and
anything it has never seen you visit waits at the bottom.
.SS While you are editing
You do not have to quit. lookit re-reads the file every time you return to the
startpage, so your changes are one keypress away.
.PP
If a line does not make sense, lookit says so across the top of the startpage,
naming the file and the line number, and ignores that one line. Nothing else in
the file is affected, and nothing is silently rewritten.
.PP
On first run, if the file does not exist, lookit creates it \(em mode 0600, in a
directory mode 0700 \(em with a short comment header explaining the format and
three starter bookmarks:
.IR jonathan@tilde.team ,
.I fingerverse@happynetbox.com
and
.IR me@andros.dev .
Delete any of those lines if you would rather not have them: an existing file
is always taken at its word, so they will not reappear. Deleting the file
itself makes lookit create it again the next time it starts.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where lookit looks for its config directory. When set, the bookmarks file is
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lookit/bookmarks ;
otherwise
.IR ~/.config/lookit/bookmarks .
.PP
lookit detects your terminal's colour support and adapts to light and dark
backgrounds on its own. It has no theme setting and no colour flag.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
lookit ran, and you quit it.
.TP
.B 1
lookit could not start: an unknown option, more than one target, or a
terminal it cannot run in. A finger lookup that fails is not an error here; it
is shown inside the browser, and you carry on.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the browser and start from the catalog:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
lookit
.EE
.RE
.PP
Open on one person, or on a host to see who is around:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
lookit jonathan@tilde.team
lookit @plan.cat
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bookmark somewhere without opening lookit at all, by adding a line to the file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
echo '@cosmic.voyage' >> ~/.config/lookit/bookmarks
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR finger (1),
.BR fingerd (8)
.PP
RFC 1288, the Finger User Information Protocol:
https://www.rfc\-editor.org/rfc/rfc1288
.PP
Bugs, ideas and patches:
https://github.com/jonathandeamer/lookit
.SH AUTHOR
Jonathan Deamer
